# graphy — code knowledge graph

This repository has a pre-built graph (`.graphy/`) of its code: functions,
methods, types, docs and config, joined by typed relationships — `calls`,
`imports`, `inherits`, `implements`, `references`, `contains`, `defines`,
`explains`, `tests`, `depends_on`. Every edge records how it was established:

- **EXTRACTED** — stated in the source and resolved unambiguously. Trust it.
- **INFERRED** — resolved through a unique global match. Usually right.
- **AMBIGUOUS** — several plausible targets. Verify before relying on it.

One query replaces many file reads. Prefer the graph for structural questions.

## The loop

Work down this list; each step narrows what you have to read.

**1. Orient** — `graphy report --sections`, then `graphy report --section
"<name>"`. Stats, the most-connected entities, and the detected subsystems.
Do this before broad architecture questions; skip it when you already know
which entity you care about.

**2. Locate** — get a foothold:

- You know the file: `graphy nodes --file path/to/file.go`
- You know the name: use it directly. Every command accepts a name
  (`CreateConcept`), a qualified name (`Store.PutFile`), a node id, or a
  repo-relative file path.
- You know neither: `graphy query "<question>"`. This is the only
  non-deterministic command here — it picks its starting points by model or
  keyword score — so use it to *find* an entity, then switch to the commands
  below.

**3. Expand** — `graphy neighbors <entity>` for what surrounds it. Narrow with
`--rel calls`, `--kind function`, `--direction out`, `--depth 2`.
`graphy explain <entity>` gives one entity in full instead: location,
signature, doc, community and its edges (same `--rel`/`--direction`/`--limit`).

**4. Assess impact** — before changing anything:

- `graphy callers <entity>` — what calls, imports, implements, tests or
  documents it. This is "what breaks if I change this".
- `graphy uses <entity>` — what it depends on.
- `graphy callers <entity> --rel tests --tests` — just its test suite.
- `graphy path <a> <b>` — how two entities connect at all.

**5. Understand intent** — `graphy why <entity>` returns the WHY:, DECISION:
and RATIONALE: comments and the architecture decision records attached to it.
Read this before changing unfamiliar code: it is the reasoning that is not in
the signature.

**6. Read** — `graphy concept <entity>` gives the wiki concept documenting it,
which `okfw read` opens. Otherwise take the location any command prints and
read the source.

**7. Refresh** — after editing, `graphy update` (incremental, sub-second), so
the next query reflects your changes.

## Bounds

Every traversal is capped and says so. A result carrying `truncated`, or
printing `… N more`, means you did **not** see everything — raise `--limit` or
narrow with `--rel`. Test entities are excluded by default, because a
well-tested function's neighbourhood is mostly its own suite; `--tests`
includes them.

## Crossing into the wiki

Entities significant enough to document are exported as OKF concepts:

- graph → wiki: `graphy concept <entity>`
- wiki → graph: `okfw search --node-id <node id>`

Use the wiki for prose, decisions and hand-written context; use the graph for
structure.

## MCP

`graphy mcp` serves the same capabilities as stdio tools: `graphy_report`,
`graphy_file_nodes`, `graphy_query`, `graphy_neighbors`, `graphy_callers`,
`graphy_explain`, `graphy_path`, `graphy_rationale`, `graphy_concept`,
`graphy_update`. Every node they return carries the `concept` documenting it,
when one exists.
